pwmodel 0

# A two-light Decorated Gothic window with plate tracery, in a stone panel.
#
# THE CONSTRUCTION. Everything here is struck with a compass from four points,
# which is how the mason set it out and why the numbers are not arbitrary:
#
#   enclosing arch   equilateral: span 130, so both arcs have radius 130 and
#                    are struck from the two springing points (-65, 165) and
#                    (65, 165). Radius = span is what makes an arch "equilateral"
#                    and fixes the apex at 165 + 130 x sin60 = 277.6.
#   each lancet      the same figure at span 48, springing 150, apex 191.6.
#   the trefoil      three foils of radius 16 whose centres sit 14 out from the
#                    eye at (0, 232), so the light closes at overall radius 30.
#
# Change the span and the apex follows it: apex = springing + span x 0.866.
# Change the springing and only the height moves.
#
# TWO RULES THIS FILE IS BUILT AROUND. Both were learned by breaking them, and
# both are about how booleans behave, not about masonry:
#
#   1. SIBLING OPS ARE APPENDED, NOT UNIONED. Two shapes written one after the
#      other inside a part are concatenated as triangles. If they overlap, the
#      faces buried inside the other shape SURVIVE, and the result is a
#      self-intersecting mesh whose interior walls z-fight through the surface.
#      Every overlapping solid here is joined with an explicit `union { }`.
#      Sibling ops are correct only for shapes that do not touch at all - the
#      balusters of a stair, the bars below.
#
#   2. SHAPES MUST OVERLAP OR CLEAR, NEVER TOUCH EXACTLY. A tool face resting
#      exactly on the face it cuts, two arcs meeting exactly at a point, a clip
#      plane through the exact corner where two circles cross - each is three
#      surfaces through one locus, and the boolean answers with zero-area
#      triangles that render as black and white shards. This is measurable:
#      three foils of radius 15 struck 15 from the eye all pass through the eye,
#      and `geometry.check_health` reports 2 degenerate triangles. Radius 16
#      struck 14 has the same overall radius 30, overlaps in an area instead of
#      a point, and reports 0. That is the only reason those two numbers are
#      what they are.
#
# So the arch head is clipped at z = 160 rather than at the springing line 165,
# and the jamb below it is 1 narrower than the head it plugs into. The 0.9 that
# leaves at the springing is not slop - it is the impost ledge, and it is what a
# reveal actually has.
#
# RESTRAINT. Three cusps, once, in the head. A trefoil says gothic; a rose of
# eight subfoils says a machine drew it. The tracery here is two lights and one
# foiled eye, which is the whole vocabulary of a village church window.

materials {
    # Slot names are the contract. Point them at your own limestone, glazing and
    # wrought iron - nothing else in the file changes.
    #
    # THREE SLOTS BECAUSE THERE ARE THREE SHADING BEHAVIOURS, which is the only
    # thing a slot boundary is for. Stone is a matte dielectric, the glazing is
    # translucent, the saddle bars are rough metal. No tint turns one into
    # another, so these three cannot collapse onto one instance the way
    # watchtower's Stone, Slate and Timber do.
    #
    # This block used to spend three different ENGINE materials for a different
    # reason: bound to one white material the glazing, the ferramenta and the
    # stone are one undifferentiated mass, the tracery cannot be read at all, and
    # the hood mould disappears into the wall it stands 14.5 proud of. That was a
    # TONAL problem being solved with whatever spread stock content happened to
    # offer. It is now solved with `color=`, against materials that sample Vertex
    # Color - and the slots are free to say what they are actually for.
    #
    # THE TWO STONE PARTS CARRY DIFFERENT TINTS IN THE SAME SLOT, which is the
    # clearest case in the corpus for having both mechanisms. `part label` - the
    # hood mould - is greyer and a shade darker than the panel it sits on. It is
    # not a different material and does not want a second slot; it is the one
    # member of this wall fully exposed to weather. The note at `part label`
    # records that at a 7 tube the mould "read as absent from every three-quarter
    # view", because a smooth white roll on a white wall has no edge to catch.
    # Projection was raised to 14.5 to fix that; the tint is the other half of the
    # same fix and costs no geometry at all.
    #
    # GLASS TAKES ITS OPACITY FROM VERTEX ALPHA, BUT NOT THE WAY THE PARAMETER NAME
    # SUGGESTS, AND THE PANE IS AUTHORED AROUND THE DIFFERENCE. The translucent
    # master computes `Opacity = VertexColor.A * OpacityFloor`, and the instance
    # sets OpacityFloor = 0.35. It is a MULTIPLIER, not a floor: alpha scales a
    # ceiling of 0.35 rather than being lifted to a minimum of it. So a = 1 is not
    # an opaque blue slab - it is the MOST opaque this pane can be, at 0.35, which
    # is about right for glazing seen from outside. Anything lower disappears.
    #
    # That is why the alpha below is 1 and not the 0.6 that reads as the sensible
    # value for glass. Read the graph before choosing a number here; the name will
    # mislead you by a factor of three.
    #
    # EVERY BOOLEAN TOOL IN `part stone` CARRIES A `color=` TOO, AND WITHOUT IT
    # THIS MODEL IS WHITE WHATEVER THE PANEL IS SET TO. `subtract` leaves the
    # TOOL'S surface behind as the new face, so the 6-deep reveal, both light
    # heads, the mullion cheeks and the trefoil are all tool geometry - the
    # tracery, which is the whole point of the model. Untinted they render pure
    # white against a limestone panel, which is exactly what "uses basic white
    # materials" was describing. The tool tint is (0.50, 0.46, 0.37), a shade
    # LIGHTER than the panel's (0.45, 0.41, 0.33): sheltered stone weathers less
    # than the face it is cut into, and 10% is enough to say so without reading
    # as a second material.
    #
    # THE VALUES ARE PUSHED FROM THE FIRST COLOUR PASS. Panel was
    # (0.74, 0.70, 0.60) and hood mould (0.63, 0.62, 0.55) - a 0.09 break between
    # two near-whites, invisible at whole-object framing. The panel is now
    # limestone rather than off-white and the mould is (0.34, 0.34, 0.31), a
    # neutral weathered grey a full value below it. Judge on a render: the
    # preview key light is worth about a stop, so these read a shade lighter on
    # screen than they do in the file.
    Stone = "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/MI_PwModelExample_MatteDielectric"
    Glass = "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/MI_PwModelExample_Glass"
    Iron  = "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/MI_PwModelExample_RoughMetal"
}

# The panel and everything cut out of it. The wall is 25 thick and the whole
# window is set out on its front face at y = -12.5.
part stone {
    box size=(180, 25, 320) at=(0, 0, 160) material="Stone" color=(0.45, 0.41, 0.33, 1)

    # Projecting sill course. It runs 10 past the panel each side because a sill
    # that stops flush with the jamb throws water onto the wall below, and it
    # goes down to z = 0 so those overhanging ends stand on something.
    #
    # `union`, not a bare second box: it shares 25 of its height with the panel,
    # and appending it would leave the buried half of each box inside the other.
    union {
        box size=(200, 40, 25) at=(0, -7.5, 12.5) color=(0.45, 0.41, 0.33, 1)
    }

    # The order of the arch: a 6-deep reveal in the shape of the enclosing arch,
    # so the arch reads in elevation as a shadow line even before the lights are
    # pierced. Two cylinders of radius 130 struck from the springing points
    # intersect in a lens; the head is that lens clipped at z = 160, and the
    # jamb is a 128-wide block plugged 12 up into it.
    #
    # The tool is 8.5 thick and sits at y = -10.75, so it spans -15 to -6.5 and
    # stands 2.5 PROUD of the panel face it cuts. A tool that stopped exactly on
    # that face at -12.5 would leave a coplanar lid over the whole reveal, and
    # that lid is the ghost patch that flickers across the opening.
    subtract {
        cylinder radius=130 height=8.5 segments=48 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(-65, -10.75, 165) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        intersection {
            cylinder radius=130 height=8.5 segments=48 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(65, -10.75, 165) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
        # Clipped at 160, not at the springing 165: the two arcs cross exactly at
        # (+-65, 165), so a plane through 165 passes through both crossings and
        # puts three surfaces through one point.
        intersection {
            box size=(300, 8.5, 300) at=(0, -10.75, 310) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
        union {
            box size=(124, 8.5, 146) at=(0, -10.75, 99) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
    }

    # The left light, pierced right through. Same figure as the enclosing arch
    # one size down: head = two radius-48 arcs struck from (-55, 150) and
    # (-7, 150), clipped at 146; jamb = a 46-wide block running up to 158, which
    # is 12 into the head and 1 clear of its edge on both sides.
    #
    # The 14 uu left between the two lights at x = -7..7 is the mullion - 140 mm
    # of stone, which is about the least that will carry an arch head.
    subtract {
        cylinder radius=48 height=60 segments=32 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(-55, 0, 150) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        intersection {
            cylinder radius=48 height=60 segments=32 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(-7, 0, 150) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
        intersection {
            box size=(300, 60, 300) at=(0, 0, 296) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
        union {
            box size=(43, 60, 113) at=(-31, 0, 101.5) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
    }

    # The right light, written out rather than mirrored.
    #
    # THE REASON THIS COMMENT USED TO GIVE IS NO LONGER TRUE. It said `mirror`
    # appends a reflection without reversing triangle winding, so a mirrored half
    # inside a subtract block is an ANTI-solid that does not cut and leaves the
    # light it should have opened as blind stone. That was a real defect and it is
    # FIXED: `GeometryOps::Mirror` passes bFixOrientationForNegativeScale, and the
    # op table now states the reversal outright. A mirrored tool cuts.
    #
    # The block still stays written out, on the reason that was always the better
    # one: an agent asked to widen ONE light edits one block, instead of unpicking
    # a symmetry it then has to preserve. Two lights of a two-light window are not
    # the same feature twice - they are the composition - and the mullion between
    # them is set by where each one stops.
    #
    # `mirror` is also the wrong SHAPE of op here even when it works. It reflects
    # the whole accumulated mesh, not the last op, so inside this block it would
    # have to be the only thing in it.
    subtract {
        cylinder radius=48 height=60 segments=32 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(7, 0, 150) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        intersection {
            cylinder radius=48 height=60 segments=32 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(55, 0, 150) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
        intersection {
            box size=(300, 60, 300) at=(0, 0, 296) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
        union {
            box size=(43, 60, 113) at=(31, 0, 101.5) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
    }

    # The foiled eye: three foils of radius 15.5 whose centres sit 15 out from
    # the eye at (0, 232), at 0, 120 and 240 degrees.
    #
    # Written out and UNIONED rather than arrayed. `array_radial` was the
    # obvious way to say "three of these", and it is wrong here for the reason
    # in the header: it APPENDS its copies, so where the foils overlap the walls
    # between them survive inside the tool, the subtraction carves them into the
    # stone, and the eye fills with a dark web instead of opening. array_radial
    # is right only for copies that do not touch - the arrow slits of a tower,
    # the merlons of a parapet.
    #
    # 15.5 struck at 15, not 15 struck at 15: the header explains the point. The
    # cusps land at 16 from the eye against an outer radius of 30.5, which is
    # what makes three lobes read as three.
    subtract {
        cylinder radius=15.5 height=60 segments=24 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(0, 0, 247) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        union {
            cylinder radius=15.5 height=60 segments=24 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(12.99, 0, 224.5) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
        union {
            cylinder radius=15.5 height=60 segments=24 rotate=(90, 0, 0) at=(-12.99, 0, 224.5) color=(0.50, 0.46, 0.37, 1)
        }
    }

    # NO `bevel` HERE, AND THE COMMENT THAT USED TO DEFEND ONE WAS EXACTLY
    # BACKWARDS. `bevel distance=1.5 segments=1` took 1.5 off every arris, on the
    # argument that "stone is never delivered with a knife edge, and the chamfer
    # is what gives the tracery a highlight instead of a flat grey face". The
    # highlight it actually gave was PURE WHITE. A chamfer is geometry a MODIFIER
    # made: no `color=` reaches it, `set_vertex_color set_all=true` does not
    # reach it either, and the result outlined both lights, the trefoil and the
    # whole reveal in white on an otherwise limestone wall - the single loudest
    # reason this model read as "a white material" at whole-object framing.
    # Without it the tracery reads as carved stone and the 6-deep reveal's own
    # shadow line does the work the chamfer was supposed to do. 3002 -> 862
    # triangles on this part. Restore it the day `bevel` output carries a colour.
    uv channel=0 mode=box scale=(0.008, 0.008)
}

# The hood mould, or label: the drip that throws rainwater clear of the head.
# It is struck from the same two springing points as the arch it follows, so it
# is concentric with the reveal rather than merely near it. Radius 142 with a 7
# tube leaves its inner edge 5 clear of the 130 reveal line, and that 5 is the
# shadow gap that stops the label reading as part of the arch. It reaches the
# centreline at 62.8 degrees, so each half must overshoot to overlap across the
# apex, which is what `union` then closes into one solid.
#
# 63.6 AND NOT 65, AND THE 1.4 DEGREES IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A CROWN AND
# A FORKED BEAK. `arch` finishes its sweep with a flat cap perpendicular to the
# tube, and past the crossing at 62.8 the two halves are already diverging: the
# tails climb ABOVE the crossing point and splay apart in x, so at 65 they ended 10
# apart with their two caps standing side by side over the join. In a shaded
# close-up that is a notched two-lobed beak with a slit down the middle, and it was
# the one part of this model that read as broken.
# At 63.6 each tail runs 0.8 degrees - about 2 units - past the crossing: still an
# overlap in area rather than at a point (rule 2 above), and now each flat cap is
# buried well inside the other tube, 2 apart against a tube 18 across. The union
# closes to the single point a hood mould over an equilateral arch actually has.
# major_steps 18 rather than 14 for the same reason: at 14 the facet either side of
# the apex was wide enough to read as a flat, and this is the one silhouette
# everybody looks at.
#
# The second half is a second `arch` yawed 180 rather than a `mirror`. The reason
# this line used to give - that a reflected half is inside out and reads as black
# patches along the mould - is stale; `mirror` reverses winding now (see the right
# light). The reason it stands on today is that `mirror` APPENDS rather than
# unions, and these two halves deliberately OVERLAP by 0.8 degrees across the
# apex. Appended, the two flat end caps would survive buried inside each other,
# which is precisely what the `union` below exists to close. A second `arch` that
# the union can consume is the op that says that.
#
# ROLL IS -90, NOT 90. `arch` sweeps in its local XY plane, from +X toward +Y
# (measured: a 90-degree arch of major 100 / minor 10 has bounds x 0..110,
# y 0..110, z -10..10). Unreal's roll is positive CLOCKWISE looking down +X, so
# roll +90 sends local +Y to world MINUS Z and the mould sweeps down and out -
# two tusks hanging off the jambs, which is exactly what it looked like. Roll
# -90 sends +Y to +Z and it climbs the arch. The cylinders elsewhere in this
# file do not care which way they were rolled, so this is the only op in the
# model where the sign is load-bearing.
# A 9 tube at y = -18, not a 7 at -16. At 7 the mould stood 10.5 proud of the
# wall and was still effectively invisible in a shaded render: a smooth white
# roll on a white wall, lit near head-on, has no edge to catch and casts almost
# no shadow, and it read as absent from every three-quarter view even though the
# geometry was there (the part's 45,929 of volume is in the merged mesh). At 9
# it projects 14.5, matching the sill's 15, and throws a shadow line down the
# wall - which is the whole point of a drip mould and the only thing that makes
# it legible.
part label {
    arch major_radius=142 minor_radius=9 angle=63.6 major_steps=18 minor_steps=8 rotate=(-90, 0, 0) at=(-65, -18, 165) material="Stone" color=(0.34, 0.34, 0.31, 1)
    union {
        arch major_radius=142 minor_radius=9 angle=63.6 major_steps=18 minor_steps=8 rotate=(-90, 0, 180) at=(65, -18, 165) color=(0.34, 0.34, 0.31, 1)
    }
    # The apex boss, and it is not decoration - it is what closes the crown.
    # Two tubes of the SAME minor radius crossing at an angle cannot be unioned into
    # a clean point, however carefully the sweep is trimmed: each half ends in a flat
    # cap perpendicular to its own tangent, and a disc of radius 9 centred barely off
    # the other tube's axis always has rim points about 0.2 outside a tube of radius
    # 9. Shortening the tails moves that sliver, never removes it. A boss placed on
    # the crossing swallows both caps whole and the arithmetic is checkable: radius 12
    # against caps whose furthest rim point is 9.8 from the crossing, and y scaled to
    # 0.95 so the 11.4 half-extent still covers the +-9 the tubes reach out of the
    # arch plane. It also happens to be what a mason would have put there.
    union {
        sphere radius=12 subdivisions=8 scale=(1, 0.95, 1) at=(0, -18, 291.3) color=(0.34, 0.34, 0.31, 1)
    }
    # The label stops: where the mould turns and dies into the wall. Each one
    # swallows the springing end of its own half, so it is a union too.
    union {
        box size=(20, 20, 20) at=(80, -18, 165) color=(0.34, 0.34, 0.31, 1)
    }
    union {
        box size=(20, 20, 20) at=(-80, -18, 165) color=(0.34, 0.34, 0.31, 1)
    }
    uv channel=0 mode=cylindrical scale=(0.02, 0.02)
}

# The glazing sits in the middle of the wall thickness, not on its face, which
# is why a rectangle is enough: the stone is opaque and 25 thick, so the pane is
# only ever seen through the lights it fills.
#
# A box 1.5 thick rather than a `plane`: a plane is one triangle layer with one
# facing, so it is invisible from behind and black wherever a light lets you see
# its back. Glass that is 15 mm of solid costs 12 triangles and is never wrong.
part glass {
    box size=(120, 1.5, 230) at=(0, 2, 155) material="Glass" color=(0.16, 0.28, 0.50, 1)
    uv channel=0 mode=box scale=(0.01, 0.01)
}

# Ferramenta: the iron saddle bars the glazing panels are tied to. They sit 1.5 in
# front of the glass, not through it - two parts that interpenetrate are two parts
# whose buried faces both survive.
#
# 124 long against lights that stop at +-55, so each bar is buried 7 into the jamb
# at both ends. The 112 they used to be reached +-56, which is 1 past the opening
# and entirely inside the 1.5 chamfer, so both ends floated free.
#
# FOUR BARS, AND `array_along_path` RATHER THAN `array_linear`, FOR TWO REASONS AND
# NEITHER OF THEM IS THE COUNT. `array_linear` would give four bars perfectly
# happily; what it cannot give is either of the things that stop them reading as
# extruded shelving:
#   * The spacing is not constant. Saddle bars divide the GLAZING, which runs from
#     z = 45 to the lancet apex at 191.6, and the divisions shorten as the head
#     closes - 68, 104, 141, 174. A constant offset is the one spacing a glazier
#     would not have used.
#   * Each bar has its own `pitch`. These are forged and bedded by hand, and under a
#     degree of tilt over 124 units is about 1.5 of drop at the end: far too little
#     to read as a mistake and just enough that the four lines are not parallel.
#     `array_linear` carries an offset and no rotation at all, so this is the only
#     op in the vocabulary that can say it. `roll` is in there too but does almost
#     nothing - a bar's long axis IS local X, so roll only spins its near-square
#     section about itself.
# The box is authored at z = 0 because the frames are ABSOLUTE part-local
# placements, not offsets from the previous copy: leaving the generator at z = 80
# would make every frame z read as a delta from it, which is exactly the habit
# `array_linear` teaches. Four frames, four bars, and the original is NOT
# additionally left standing at z = 0.
part ferramenta {
    box size=(124, 3, 2.5) at=(0, -1, 0) material="Iron" color=(0.08, 0.06, 0.05, 1)
    array_along_path path=[(0, 0, 68, 3, 0.7, 0), (0, 0, 104, -2, -0.5, 0), (0, 0, 141, 4, 0.9, 0), (0, 0, 174, -3, -0.6, 0)]
    uv channel=0 mode=box scale=(0.05, 0.05)
}

# Explicit elements, not `auto`, and not nothing - which is what this file shipped
# with until now. A model with no `collision` block compiles to zero simple elements
# and CTF_UseDefault, so a consumer gets whatever the project default is and a
# character walks through the wall.
#
# THE OPENINGS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT MODELLED IN THE SIMPLE HULLS. Simple collision
# is convex primitives and convex primitives cannot have holes, so the only way to
# leave the two lights and the eye open would be to build the tracery out of a
# dozen boxes - which costs more than it buys for a wall panel that is meant to be
# walked past. `simple_and_complex` is the answer: gameplay hits the two boxes, and
# anything that needs the light to actually be a hole (a line trace for visibility,
# a projectile) asks for complex and gets the real render mesh. The model's own
# triangle count is deliberately not written here - it moves whenever this file is
# reworked; the dated corpus reading is in docs/wiki-src/model.examples.md.
#
# The hood mould stands 14.5 proud of the panel face and is left to complex
# collision for the same reason: boxing an arch costs four elements to cover a
# feature nothing collides with.
collision {
    complexity = simple_and_complex
    box size=(180, 25, 320) at=(0, 0, 160)
    box size=(200, 40, 25) at=(0, -7.5, 12.5)
}
